========================================================================
3. HIGH-SNR vs LOW-SNR CRITERION (production data, current chain)
========================================================================
  Observable production state: weight_mode=1 (equal weight). Every accepted sample
  gets w=1, so w_highSNR == w_lowSNR == 1 -> the criterion 'w_high >= w_low' holds
  trivially, but ONLY because there is no variance surface at all (uncertainty_available
  = false). There is nothing to propagate: the normalization emitted no variance and
  the input frames have no ivar product.

  Consequence of the missing propagation on the SNR weight chain (if it were closed):
      highest-SNR  SNR=47.388 F_ref=11883.0  w(common F)=1.876175e-04  w(own F)=1.590338e-05  ratio=11.7973
      lowest-SNR   SNR=21.364 F_ref=1728.8  w(common F)=3.813288e-05  w(own F)=1.527050e-04  ratio=0.2497
  -> using the frame's own F_ref collapses w to ~1/sigma_F^2 and silently reduces the
     high-SNR frame's common-scale weight by a_f^2; the chain currently fail-closes
     instead (safe), but any relaxation of the 1e-9 gate would expose the down-weighting.
